Just thought I'd play Devil's Advocate for a change here:

It seems to be granted that if a monster is generated with a
wand of fire in his hand, he knows that it's a wand of fire.
Now, being of an intelligent race (not to mention an entire of
civilization of Gnomes living in the mines), how does he know
this particular wand of fire?

Because his cultural heritage says "this is a wand of fire" and
"this is what a wand of fire looks like".  Just as we know as
player characters, if one glass wand is a wand of fire, then
all glass wands are wands of fire.

Now, wands of fire don't grow on trees.  Only a few gnomes
are allocated a wand of fire when they are sent out to defend
the home against these nasty human intruders from the surface.
Even so, each defender has lived in his home his whole life,
and undoubtedly seen a wand of fire lying about someplace,
and being intelligent, he'd be able to know one when he sees one.

Therefore, as he hears you coming he glances around the room
and sees a glass wand in the corner.  "Hey, a glass wand!
Fred had one of those and it shot flames at people.  I bet
this'll do the same thing!"  And so your gnome goes over,
picks up a wand he didn't start with, and proceeds to zap
it at you.

This angle seems to be entirely consistent with how the game
is currently coded.  If an intelligent race sees a wand of
a type they tend to use (same thing for potions and scrolls)
they will try to use it.  Other things they tend to ignore
(or so I've been led to believe.  I don't think I've seen
monsters with scrolls of charging, or wands of secret door
detection).  If that is the case, then you can guess things
about items based on whether the monster seems interested.

You see the hobbit chase after the wand and pick it up.
You magic missile him before he uses the wand, and think:
it could be offensive (sleep, fire, cold, magic missile)
it could be defensive (teleport, digging)
it won't be (cancellation, locking, door detection, wishing)

You see a gnome walk over a scroll without bothering to pick it up.
It isn't scare monster (he can't walk on that)
It isn't create monster or teleportation.

In retrospect, this can lead to a rather subtle game, in
which one step in guessing at a scroll is based on whether
an intelligent monster expresses any interest in it.
A skilled player would try to allow the monster to live
long enough to make a decision, but make sure it is dead
before it gets a chance to use any magic item.

I've had my share of being on the bad end of monsters
using items.  I've learned to live with it; your tactics
just have to adjust from the brute force attack-anything
that-moves.

